Exercise 2.13 asks to prove that the elementwise maps \(f\colon|D_0|\to|D_1|\) coming from approximable mappings are exactly the continuous maps of the domain topologies, using the inverse-image formula of Exercise 2.9. `example_213` is the conjunction of `continuous_toElementMap`, `mem_iff_principal_of_continuous`, and `toElementMap_ofContinuous`. In the forward direction `continuous_toElementMap` takes an open containing \(f(x)\), extracts a neighbourhood of the image lying in that open, unfolds the approximable relation to a neighbourhood \(X\) of \(x\), and concludes that the basic open \([X]\) sits in the preimage; that is the basis reading of the note's identity \(f^{-1}[Y]=\bigcup\{[X]\mid Y\in f(\uparrow X)\}\). The converse criterion is `mem_iff_principal_of_continuous`, namely \(Y\in c(x)\) if and only if \(Y\in c(\uparrow X)\) for some \(X\in x\), which is the note's displayed equivalence on the basic open \([Y]\): the forward step uses that the preimage of \([Y]\) is open and therefore contains a basic neighbourhood of a token of \(x\), and the reverse step transports the neighbourhood by `continuous_monotone` along \(\uparrow X\sqsubseteq x\). `principalFamily_directed` and `eq_iSupDirected_principal` are the note's decomposition \(x=\bigcup\{\uparrow X\mid X\in x\}\) as a directed supremum of principal elements. `ofContinuous` is the monotone extension of \(X\mapsto c(\uparrow X)\), using that principal elements reverse inclusion, and `toElementMap_ofContinuous` shows that the approximable union formula and this continuous union formula pick out the same neighbourhoods, so the induced element map is \(c\). `continuous_of_monotone_iSupDirected` is a further characterization of continuity and is not a conjunct of `example_213`. **Theorem:** yes — approximable element maps are exactly the continuous maps, as the three conjuncts of `example_213`. **Proof:** yes — the basis argument, the neighbourhood form of the hint, the directed decomposition into principal elements, and the round trip through `ofContinuous` are the steps above; the continuity direction is read off the relation and the basic opens rather than by citing the inverse-image identity of Exercise 2.9.
